.\"\t$NetBSD$
.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt SNESPAD 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm snespad
.Nd SNES gamepad driver for spi(4) buses
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "snespad* at spi? slave ?"
.Cd "wskbd* at wskbddev? mux 1"
.Pp
In a kernel configuration file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
.Cd "snespad* at spi? slave 0"
.Cd "snespad* at spi? slave 1"
.Cd "wskbd* at wskbddev? mux 1"
.Ed
.Pp
The driver is also available as a loadable module
.Pq Pa snespad.kmod .
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver supports original SNES gamepads connected to a
.Xr spi 4
bus.  The pad's Latch line is wired to a chip select (slave) of the bus,
Clock to SCLK, Data to MISO, and the pad is powered from +5V.
This is the wiring of the
.Lk https://github.com/thgrass/SNES-Gamepads-to-SPI-Adaptor "SNES-Gamepads-to-SPI-Adaptor"
board (two pads on one bus, one per chip select, using per-socket
tri-state buffers on the shared MISO line), or a single pad wired
directly to any SPI port without any extra chip.
.Pp
On attach, and then at about 60 Hz from a kernel thread, the driver
asserts the slave's chip select and clocks out 16 SPI mode-0 bits,
MSB first, at 500 kHz.  The pad reports its 12 buttons active-low in the
order B, Y, Select, Start, Up, Down, Left, Right, A, X, L, R, followed by
four one-bits; an idle pad therefore reads raw 0xffff.  A pad is only
trusted when the trailing four bits are ones
.Pq raw & 0xf == 0xf ;
an empty socket (floating data line) is reported as "no buttons" so it
cannot generate phantom events.
.Pp
Up to four pads are supported (one per chip select,
.Cd "snespad* at spi? slave N" ,
N = 0..3; the default module config enables N = 0 and 1, the driver's
.Dv SNESPAD_MAX_PORTS
compile-time knob defaults to 4).  Each attached pad is exposed as a
.Xr wskbd 4
keyboard so wscons/X11 applications and emulators see it as a plain
keyboard.  To keep several pads on the same keyboard distinguishable,
each port maps its buttons to a different key set:
.Bl -tag -width "port 3" -compact
.It port 0 (slave 0)
D-pad = arrow keys, A=a, B=b, X=x, Y=y, L=l, R=r, Start=Return, Select=Space
.It port 1 (slave 1)
numeric keypad (D-pad = KP 8/2/4/6, A=KP 1, B=KP 3, X=KP 9, Y=KP 7,
Start=KP Enter, Select=KP 0, L=KP -, R=KP +)
.It port 2 (slave 2)
F1..F12
.It port 3 (slave 3)
keys c, d, e, f, g, h, i, j, k, m, n, o
.El
.Pp
The wskbd child requires
.Cd "wskbd* at wskbddev? mux 1"
in the kernel configuration (this is the standard attribute-based
attach; the driver provides the
.Dv wskbddev
attribute).
.Pp
Debug output is available via
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysctl -w hw.snespad_debug=1    # log button changes
# sysctl -w hw.snespad_debug=2    # log every sample
.Ed
.Pp
and appears in
.Xr dmesg 8 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr spi 4 ,
.Xr wskbd 4 ,
.Xr modload 8 ,
.Xr MAKEDEV 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Nx 11.1 .
It attaches to any
.Xr spi 4
bus via
.Cd "snespad* at spi? slave N" .
When loaded at runtime on a stock
.Xr spi 4
(bus without ca_rescan), the spi(4) rescan fix is required for the
attach to happen; built into the kernel config directly, no extra patch is needed.
